## Change

Added one new file, `src/main/java/com/example/ordering/OrderRepositoryMixin.java`:

```java
package com.example.ordering;

import be.appify.prefab.core.annotations.RepositoryMixin;
import java.util.List;

@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)
public interface OrderRepositoryMixin {
    List<Order> findByCustomerEmail(String customerEmail);

    long countByStatus(OrderStatus status);
}
```

## Why this is the right mechanism

`Order` is a Prefab `@Aggregate`, so `com.example.ordering.application.OrderRepository` is **generated** by the Prefab annotation processor (it can't be hand-edited — it's regenerated on every build). Prefab's way to add methods to that generated repository is a **`@RepositoryMixin`** interface, which is exactly the project's name (`mixin-derived-query`). Prefab itself demonstrates this in `prefab-demo` with `be.appify.prefab.demo.user.UserRepositoryMixin`, which lives in the aggregate's own package and is annotated `@RepositoryMixin(User.class)`.

After rebuilding, the processor now emits the repository as:

```java
public interface OrderRepository
        extends CrudRepository<Order, String>,
                PagingAndSortingRepository<Order, String>,
                OrderRepositoryMixin {
}
```

So the two derived queries become part of the single `OrderRepository` that other beans inject — no second repository, no `@Query` needed since `findByCustomerEmail`/`countByStatus` resolve cleanly against `Order`'s existing `customerEmail` and `status` properties.

## Verification

- `mvn clean test` → **BUILD SUCCESS**; the 4 main sources (including the mixin) and the 6 generated test-scaffolding sources all compile.
- Confirmed the regenerated `OrderRepository.java` now `extends OrderRepositoryMixin`.
- The starter ships no unit tests, so the suite passes (nothing fails).

One thing I could **not** do in this environment: run a live Spring integration test of the queries. Surefire's forked JVM can't create its temp dir (`/var/folders/.../T/surefire-stijnvanbael`) under the sandbox's filesystem restrictions, so any `@SpringBootTest` fails to boot the fork with `IOException: Unable to create temporary directory` — an environment constraint, not a code issue. I therefore verified the change structurally (clean compile + regenerated source) rather than at runtime. If you'd like, run `mvn test` outside the sandbox to exercise the queries against Prefab's in-memory/container test infra.

The implementation is complete and verified. Here's a summary of what I did.

## What I added

**`src/main/java/com/example/planning/Task.java`** — a new Prefab aggregate nested under `Project`:

```java
@Aggregate
@GetById
@GetList
public record Task(
        @Id Reference<Task> id,
        @Version long version,
        @Parent @NotNull Reference<Project> parent,
        @NotNull String title,
        boolean done
) {
    @Create
    public Task(Reference<Project> parent, @NotNull String title) {
        this(Reference.create(), 0L, parent, title, false);   // done defaults to false
    }

    @Update(method = "POST", path = "/complete")
    public Task complete() {
        return new Task(this.id(), this.version(), this.parent(), this.title(), true);
    }
}
```

The key insight from studying the Prefab annotation processor was that `@Parent` (a marker on a `Reference` field) makes the aggregate a sub-resource: its REST paths get nested under the parent's path, and the parent id is bound from the path rather than the request body. The processor then generates the controller, service, repository, request/response, and paged query automatically.

## Endpoints produced (verified in generated `TaskController`)

| Endpoint | Result |
|---|---|
| `POST /projects/{parentId}/tasks` with `{"title":...}` | `201 Created`, `Location` ending in the task id |
| `GET /projects/{parentId}/tasks/{id}` | `200` with the task JSON (`id`, `title`, `done`, …) |
| `GET /projects/{parentId}/tasks` | `200` with a Spring Data `PagedModel` (`content` array), filtered to that project |
| `POST /projects/{parentId}/tasks/{id}/complete` | `200 OK`, sets `done = true` |

One subtlety: `@Update`'s `path` is concatenated onto `/{id}`, so `path = "complete"` produced `/{id}complete`. Using `path = "/complete"` yields the required `/{id}/complete`.

## Verification

I added `src/test/java/com/example/planning/TaskRestVerificationTest.java`, a `@SpringBootTest` that drives the full lifecycle through the Prefab-generated `ProjectClient`/`TaskClient` (in-memory mode via `prefab.test.containers=false`): create project → create task → get (asserts `done == false`) → list (asserts the content contains the task) → complete → get (asserts `done == true`).

`mvn clean test` → **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The project compiles and all tests pass.

(The `ForkStarter … Unable to create temporary directory` lines in the log are a sandbox temp-dir restriction on surefire's fork; the test still ran in-process and passed, and they won't occur in a normal environment.)

Done. The validation is in place and verified end-to-end.

## What I did

**`src/main/java/com/example/crm/Customer.java`** — added Bean Validation constraints to the `@Create` constructor parameters of the `Customer` aggregate:

```java
@Create
public Customer(
        @NotBlank @Size(max = 100) String name,
        @NotBlank @Email String email
) {
    this(Reference.create(), 0L, name, email);
}
```

## How it works

Prefab generates the REST layer for `POST /customers` from the `@Create` constructor. It already wires `@Valid @RequestBody CreateCustomerRequest request` on the generated controller, so it propagates the `jakarta.validation` constraints from the constructor onto the generated `CreateCustomerRequest` DTO. I confirmed the regenerated DTO now carries `@NotBlank @Size(max=100)` on `name` and `@NotBlank @Email` on `email`. Spring's `@Valid` triggers Bean Validation before the service runs, so an invalid body returns `400 Bad Request` and nothing is persisted; a valid body still returns `201 Created`.

- `name` → `@NotBlank` (required + not blank) + `@Size(max = 100)` (length cap)
- `email` → `@NotBlank` (required, non-empty) + `@Email` (well-formed address)

## Tests

Added `src/test/java/com/example/crm/CustomerCreateValidationTest.java` (`@SpringBootTest`, profile `test`) covering: valid request → `201`; blank name, missing name, name >100 chars, missing email, and malformed email → `400`.

A clean `mvn clean test` builds and passes all 6 tests (0 failures, 0 errors).
